<article class="env-lemma" id="06RS">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06RS">Lemma <span data-tag="06RS">10.153.10</span>.</a> Let $(R, \mathfrak m)$ be a local ring of dimension $0$. Then $R$ is henselian. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $R \to S$ be a finite ring map. By Lemma <a href="/tag/04GG" data-tag="04GG">10.153.3</a> it suffices to show that $S$ is a product of local rings. By Lemma <a href="/tag/05DR" data-tag="05DR">10.36.21</a> $S$ has finitely many primes $\mathfrak m_1, \ldots , \mathfrak m_ r$ which all lie over $\mathfrak m$. There are no inclusions among these primes, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00GT" data-tag="00GT">10.36.20</a>, hence they are all maximal. Every element of $\mathfrak m_1 \cap \ldots \cap \mathfrak m_ r$ is nilpotent by Lemma <a href="/tag/00E0" data-tag="00E0">10.17.2</a>. It follows $S$ is the product of the localizations of $S$ at the primes $\mathfrak m_ i$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/00JA" data-tag="00JA">10.53.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>